<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>What does an NRR over 100% signify?</h3>
    <p>An NRR above 100% means your existing customer cohort generated more revenue growth through account expansion than you lost from downgrades and cancellations combined. This is known as net negative revenue churn.</p>

    <h3>Can Gross Revenue Retention exceed 100%?</h3>
    <p>No. Because GRR ignores expansion revenue and only accounts for downgrades and churn, GRR has a maximum theoretical value of 100%.</p>

    <h3>Why do enterprise software valuations prioritize NRR?</h3>
    <p>High NRR proves strong product utility and expanding enterprise usage. Investors value companies with high NRR because account expansion compounds recurring revenue over time without incurring additional acquisition overhead.</p>
</section>

